<div class="def"><dt>active waiting</dt><dd>Staying open to possibilities while continuing the work of inquiry. Goggin and Goggin connect active waiting to the “prepared mind” needed to recognize unexpected discovery (4).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>career arc</dt><dd>A linear story of academic introduction, ascent, achievement, and eventual decline. Pinkert and Bowen argue that this model can cast real careers involving interruption, caregiving, contingent work, or return as “deviant or deficient” (27).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>conduction</dt><dd>This project’s mentorship adaptation of Zweig’s lightning-conductor image: helping intellectual, creative, ethical, and affective intensity move into inquiry, craft, evidence, and form without predetermining the scholar who must emerge (Zweig 289–90).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>daemon / daimonic</dt><dd>Zweig calls the daimonic “the unrest that is in us all,” an unrest that can push beyond the familiar toward the “dangerous realm of inquiry”; he also warns that excessive daimonic force can “snatch[] the helm” (243–45).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>disciplinary lifecycling</dt><dd>Pinkert and Bowen’s alternative to the career arc: academic and disciplinary development as iterative, overlapping, interruptible, and distributed across multiple roles and sites (16, 27–36).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>epistemic injustice</dt><dd>Harm to a person in their capacity as a knower. Godbee uses the concept to examine how graduate writers’ authority and credibility can be unequally recognized (43–44).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>epistemic rights</dt><dd>Rights to “knowledge, experience, or earned expertise.” Godbee argues that educators and administrators have responsibilities to affirm writers’ rights to know, relate, report, claim, and act from that knowledge and experience (43–44).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>legitimate peripheral participation</dt><dd>A model of learning in which newcomers move from peripheral toward fuller participation in a community of practice. Teeuwsen, Ratković, and Tilley trouble the model’s implied endpoint because becoming an academic is “rarely finished” (684).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>liminality</dt><dd>A threshold condition of being between positions, identities, times, genres, or forms of recognition. In this project, liminality is multiple, moving, and unequal rather than a single temporary bridge between two stable shores (Godbee 43–44; Ives and Spitzer; Teeuwsen et al. 684).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>state / a state</dt><dd>This project uses <em>state</em> in two linked senses: a settled condition and an institutional force of naming, classifying, evaluating, credentialing, and making subjects legible. The university is not being equated with the nation-state; the term marks the pressure toward a recognizable academic identity (Ellsworth 6, 47).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>serendipity</dt><dd>Not passive luck but unexpected discovery made possible through preparation, openness, attention, history, environment, and the ability to recognize significance when it appears (Goggin and Goggin 4; Koupf 54–55).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>trajectory entity</dt><dd>Through Jay Lemke, Wetherbee Phelps describes a human being as not definable at one instant but only across a lifespan trajectory developing through interactions with changing environments (14).</dd></div>
<div class="def"><dt>witness</dt><dd>In Ram Dass, an observing position that notices without immediately evaluating. This project adapts witnessing as a graduate-writing practice: notice what keeps returning before deciding whether it is brilliant, foolish, or failure (Dass 75, 80).</dd></div>
</dl>
</aside>

<aside class="drawer" id="practices" aria-label="Little movements">
<button class="drawer-close" data-close>Close</button><h2>Little Movements</h2>
<div class="practice-intro"><p><strong>Not remedies. Not a universal graduate-school model.</strong> These are smaller practices and orientations that might make room for different forms of becoming with/in the institution.</p></div>
<div class="practice-cards">
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--skin-1)"><h3>1 · Notice</h3><p>Notice before immediately judging. Put intuition beside the intellect rather than beneath it or in place of it. Attend to the question, source, sentence, resistance, or relation that keeps returning (Dass 75, 80).</p></div>
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--skin-2)"><h3>2 · Wait</h3><p>Remain open while continuing to do the work. Serendipity requires both preparation and an open mind able to recognize what was not planned. “Proceed as way opens” (Goggin and Goggin 4; Koupf 54–55; Phelps 2).</p></div>
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--skin-3)"><h3>3 · Accompany</h3><p>Do not make graduate students inhabit uncertainty alone. Godbee’s feminist co-mentoring models “power with”: listening, affirming, questioning, and helping writers reclaim epistemic rights (44–48).</p></div>
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--skin-4)"><h3>4 · Mark</h3><p>Recognize movement before graduation. Mark pivots, returns, recoveries, changed methods, abandoned arguments, and other movements that do not look like linear ascent (Pinkert and Bowen 16, 27–36; Phelps 14).</p></div>
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--skin-5)"><h3>5 · Care</h3><p>Make intellectual risk materially possible. Care cannot be reduced to self-care instructions; it has to take material form through time, support, flexibility, access, rest, funding, and reasonable workloads.</p></div>
<div class="practice-card" style="--tone:var(--gray-2)"><h3>6 · Allow</h3><p>Stop treating unfinishedness automatically as deficiency. A requirement can end without finishing the person, the question, the discipline, or the academic life (Phelps 14; Pinkert and Bowen 16, 27–36; Teeuwsen et al. 684).</p></div>
</div>
</aside>

<aside class="drawer" id="sources" aria-label="Sources and Works Cited">
<button class="drawer-close" data-close>Close</button><h2>Sources / Constellation</h2>
<div class="source-group"><h3>Academic becoming / route</h3><p>Teeuwsen, Ratković, and Tilley trouble the newcomer-to-oldtimer trajectory; Ives and Spitzer show scholarly expertise becoming novicehood again in unfamiliar genres; Pinkert and Bowen replace the career arc with disciplinary lifecycling.</p></div>
<div class="source-group"><h3>Recognition / power / relation</h3><p>Godbee names epistemic injustice and epistemic rights and offers feminist co-mentoring as relational “power with”; Ellsworth shows how pedagogical address positions an imagined student before that student can answer back.</p></div>
<div class="source-group"><h3>Time / lifespan</h3><p>Louise Wetherbee Phelps theorizes people as dynamic systems and, through Lemke, as trajectory entities whose becoming cannot be read from one instant.</p></div>
<div class="source-group"><h3>Creative unrest / intuition / serendipity</h3><p>Zweig supplies the daimonic and lightning-conductor images; Ram Dass supplies the witness and movement from intellect to intuition; Goggin and Goggin and Koupf provide language for active waiting, chance, preparation, and distributed invention.</p></div>
<h3>Works Cited</h3>
<div class="wc">
<p>Dass, Ram. <em>Paths to God: Living the Bhagavad Gita</em>. Three Rivers Press, 2004.</p>
<p>Ellsworth, Elizabeth. <em>Teaching Positions: Difference, Pedagogy, and the Power of Address</em>. Teachers College Press, 1997.</p>
<p>Godbee, Beth. “The Trauma of Graduate Education: Graduate Writers Countering Epistemic Injustice and Reclaiming Epistemic Rights.” <em>Learning from the Lived Experiences of Graduate Student Writers</em>, edited by Alexandria Lockett et al., Utah State UP, 2020, pp. 35–51.</p>
<p>Goggin, Maureen Daly, and Peter N. Goggin. “Stumbling into Wisdom in Rhetoric, Writing, and Literacy Research: An Introduction.” <em>Serendipity in Rhetoric, Writing, and Literacy Research</em>, edited by Maureen Daly Goggin and Peter N. Goggin, Utah State UP, 2018, pp. 3–14.</p>
<p>Ives, Lindsey, and Linnea Spitzer. “Student to Scholar: Mentorship, Recontextualization, and the Threshold of Scholarly Publication in Rhetoric and Composition.” <em>Composition Forum</em>, no. 51, 2023, compositionforum.com/issue/51/mentorship.php.</p>
<p>Koupf, Danielle. “Where Chance and Invention Collide: Scrap Writing in the Composition Classroom.” <em>Composition Studies</em>, vol. 53, no. 2, 2025, pp. 54–69.</p>
<p>Phelps, Louise Wetherbee. “Bouncing Back: Resilience and Its Limits in Late-Age Composing.” <em>Literacy in Composition Studies</em>, vol. 10, no. 2, 2023, pp. 1–37. doi:10.21623/1.10.2.2.</p>
<p>Pinkert, Laurie A., and Lauren Marshall Bowen. “Disciplinary Lifecycling: A Generative Framework for Career Trajectories in Rhetoric, Composition, and Writing Studies.” <em>Composition Studies</em>, vol. 49, no. 1, 2021, pp. 16–41.</p>
<p>Teeuwsen, Phil, Snežana Ratković, and Susan A. Tilley. “Becoming Academics: Experiencing Legitimate Peripheral Participation in Part-Time Doctoral Studies.” <em>Studies in Higher Education</em>, vol. 39, no. 4, 2014, pp. 680–94. doi:10.1080/03075079.2012.729030.</p>
<p>Zweig, Stefan. <em>Hölderlin, Kleist, and Nietzsche: The Struggle with the Daemon</em>. With a new introduction by Laurence Mintz, Routledge, 2017. Originally published 1939.</p>
</div>
<p class="small">Page citations to Zweig in this project refer to the Routledge edition listed here.</p>
</aside>

<aside class="drawer" id="about" aria-label="About and reuse">
<button class="drawer-close" data-close>Close</button><h2>About / Reuse</h2>
<p>This is a scholarly performance whose medium is a website. The spoken talk supplies the lived through-line; the first five movements make large institutional patterns visible; the daemon and conduction screens turn the scale; six little movements offer smaller practices; audience fragments alter the route, timeline, state, and poem in real time; the performance remains unfinished after the speaker stops.</p>
<h3>Shared / local participation</h3><p><strong>Shared mode:</strong> when this site is served by the included Node server, audience fragments are shared live across devices in the same room code. The server stores fragment text, prompt type, and time only; it does not ask for names, emails, or accounts.</p><p><strong>Local mode:</strong> if opened as a plain HTML file or from static hosting without the included API, contributions remain in that browser only. The rest of the performance still works.</p>
<h3>Three performance modes</h3><div class="mode-help"><strong>PRESENT</strong> — speaker-controlled movement through the 15-screen argument. Use <code>?room=rmmla2026&amp;presenter=1</code> or <code>&amp;mode=present</code>.<br><strong>LOOP</strong> — an ambient, continuing sequence for questions, before/after the session, or installation display. Press <strong>L</strong> to start or stop it. New contributions continue entering the project.<br><strong>EXPLORE</strong> — visitor-controlled navigation, Definitions, Sources / Works Cited, and participation.</div>
<h3>Use it again</h3><p>Keep the scholarly site stable and change only the room code, for example <code>?room=rmmla2026</code> or <code>?room=campus-talk</code>. Each room creates a distinct evolving poem rather than mixing events together.</p>
<h3>Presenter controls</h3><p>Keyboard: ← / → moves between sections; L starts/stops the ambient loop; P toggles presenter notes; C opens the contribution form; D opens Definitions; O opens Practices; S opens Sources; M opens presenter moderation; Escape exits the loop into PRESENT mode.</p>
